SETLOCALE(3)                   P32W Unix                    SETLOCALE(3)

NAME
       setlocale, localeconv - the locale (only "C")

SYNOPSIS
       #include <locale.h>

       char *setlocale(int category, const char *locale);
       struct lconv *localeconv(void);

DESCRIPTION
       P32W Unix has one locale, "C": ASCII characters, '.' as the
       decimal point, no grouping of digits, and strcoll compares
       as strcmp does. These are here so that programs written for
       other systems compile and run.

       setlocale with locale "C", "POSIX" or "" (the environment's
       choice) selects "C"; with NULL it asks which locale is in
       use. category (LC_ALL, LC_COLLATE, LC_CTYPE, LC_MONETARY,
       LC_NUMERIC, LC_TIME) makes no difference.

       localeconv describes the locale's numbers: decimal_point is
       ".", the other strings are empty, and the char fields are
       127 (CHAR_MAX: not given).

RETURN VALUE
       setlocale returns "C", or NULL for any other locale.
       localeconv returns a static struct lconv.

SEE ALSO
       strcmp(3), printf(3)
